Inter-Regular.ttf, Inter-Medium.ttf, Inter-SemiBold.ttf: Inter 4.1, Copyright 2016 The Inter
Project Authors (https://github.com/rsms/inter), SIL Open Font License 1.1
(https://openfontlicense.org), from Fedora's rsms-inter-fonts package, modified: the 22 characters
the emoji font also draws (U+203C, 2049, 2122, 2194-2199, 21A9, 21AA, 23CF, 24C2, 25AA, 25B6,
25C0, 2600, 2665, 26A0, 2764, 2B06, 2B1C) are removed, so a heart in a message is the colour
emoji and not Inter's outline. Made with fontTools' subsetter: every other character, all layout
features and names kept.

Compiled into every build as the app's face: a system face may be one variable font, which the
text shaper does not embolden, and its weights then all draw alike.

NotoColorEmoji.ttf: Noto Color Emoji 2.042 (CBDT), copied from gpui-mobile's
example app. SIL Open Font License 1.1, https://openfontlicense.org.
Android 13+ ships only a COLRv1 emoji font, which GPUI cannot draw, so the app adds this
one itself (`emoji::use_bundled`). It is not an APK asset: gpui-mobile would load it on its own, and a face with an 'm' could not be dropped for the user's chosen font.

The committed NotoColorEmoji.ttf is a modified copy: the upstream file went through
`emoji::import` (src/emoji.rs), which appends a `name` table (family "Noto Color Emoji",
PostScript "NotoColorEmoji") and a `cmap` with a Latin 'm', so requesting the family keeps it.
Noto Color Emoji has no Reserved Font Name, so the name stays. Made with:
MAKE_BUNDLED_SRC=<upstream.ttf> MAKE_BUNDLED_OUT=<existing dir> cargo test --lib make_bundled_font -- --ignored
and the file `emoji-font` there copied over this one. The test the_bundled_font_is_ready_to_be_requested checks it.
